Interpretation:

- **`<git.user>`** = local part of `git config user.email` (before `@`), e.g. `igor.cwer`. Must still be validated as a legal refname component.
- **Short task name** = task id when it is a kebab custom id; otherwise a slug derived from the title (design decides details).
- **Start point** is recorded on the task at `start_task` (start commit SHA and the base branch it was started from) rather than recomputed via merge-base.
- **Subtasks** work in a sub-branch of the parent's wip branch. A subtask branch is only created if work on the subtask is actually started. Before the parent can be completed, every started subtask must be resolved and its work merged into the parent's wip branch. Note: git refs cannot have both `a/b` and `a/b/c` (file/directory conflict), so the subtask branch naming must avoid nesting under the parent's ref path.
- **Delivered completion** (`completed`): squash everything since the start commit (including uncommitted changes and the task's own final status write) into one commit on `<user>/<name>`; keep the wip branch intact; stay checked out on `<user>/<name>` afterwards. Merging into main or opening a PR is the user's job.
- **Non-delivered completion** (`obsolete`, `superseded`, `duplicate`, `wontfix`): no squash; leave the wip branch as is (any uncommitted work must be preserved, not discarded) and switch back to the base branch the task was started from.
- **This task (`git-branch-per-task`) is done the old way**, without the new branching (it was started on `main_patched` before the feature existed).
- **Web UI**: show the task's branch as a simple label with a copy-to-clipboard control (card and/or detail view).
- ~~`.current_task` stored in the branch~~ — superseded, see second round below.
- A dedicated skill is acceptable only as a thin caller (e.g. supplying the commit message); all git operations are performed by the MCP server.
- The feature is opt-in via `mcp-tasks.yaml` so existing users are unaffected.

