## Problem statement

`start_task` and `complete_task` must, when enabled, manage a per-task git branch pair inside the MCP server — creating and checking out `<user>/wip/<name>` from the current HEAD on start, and on completion producing a single squashed commit of everything done since that start point on `<user>/<name>` — without ever losing committed or uncommitted work.

## Acceptance criteria

- Branching is opt-in via config; with it disabled, `start_task`/`complete_task` behave exactly as today.
- `start_task` on an enabled project creates `<user>/wip/<name>` from the current HEAD, checks it out, and records the start commit on the task.
- `complete_task` creates `<user>/<name>` whose tree equals the wip tip (plus any uncommitted changes, per the design decision) and whose history is `start commit + exactly one commit`.
- The wip branch and all its commits remain reachable after completion; no uncommitted change is discarded at any step.
- Every git failure (not a repo, dirty tree conflicts, branch already exists, detached HEAD, missing start commit, ...) returns a clear tool error and leaves the repository and the task status unchanged (no half-applied state).
- Git operations run in the resolved project root, not the server's cwd.
- Subtasks get sub-branches of the parent's wip branch when started; the parent cannot complete until started subtasks are resolved and merged into the parent's wip branch.
- A non-delivered resolution leaves the wip branch untouched (no squash, no lost uncommitted work) and checks out the base branch.
- After a delivered completion the repository is left on `<user>/<name>`.
- The web UI shows the task branch as a label with copy-to-clipboard.
- The current-task pointer lives at `<tasks_dir>/.users/<user>/current_task`, is written by the server, and skills read it through the server.
- Re-running `start_task` on a task whose wip branch exists switches to it and rebases it onto its parent branch; a rebase conflict aborts cleanly with no data loss.
- Top-level tasks branch from the first existing branch of a configurable priority list (default `main_patched`, `master_patched`, `main`, `master`).
- Works whether the tasks directory is in the code repo, in a separate repo, or untracked.
- Behaviour is covered by tests against real temporary git repositories.
- CLAUDE.md / README / server instructions document the feature.
